IV. Post-Retirement Work: 1979–2011
Upon retirement, Gunderson established Ted Gunderson International Inc., a private investigative consultancy based in Los Angeles. 
The firm drew on his extensive law enforcement network and operated at the professional level — retained by corporations, defense attorneys, 
and private clients pursuing complex matters the official system had not resolved.
His trajectory shifted materially in 1980 when he was engaged by the defense team of Dr. Jeffrey MacDonald, 
a former Army physician convicted of murdering his pregnant wife and two daughters. 
Gunderson approached the case as a structured investigative review. In the course of that work, he located and interviewed Helena Stoeckley, 
a witness who provided accounts implicating an external group in the crimes. 
The courts did not accept the alternative suspect theory as sufficient to overturn the conviction, 
but the investigation produced documented leads that kept the case in active legal contention for decades.
That engagement introduced Gunderson to a category of case — disputed convictions, 
unresolved deaths, and institutional failures — that would define the remainder of his professional life. 
Throughout the 1980s and 1990s, 
he accepted cases that families and individuals brought to him after official channels had closed or been unresponsive.

